            <ArticleTitle>Thin Glomerular Basement Membrane in a Kidney Transplant of an Alport's Syndrome Patient: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2384-2387</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0041-1345(17)30682-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.transproceed.2017.07.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Alport syndrome (AS) and thin basement membrane lesions are caused by various mutations in type IV collagen genes. Although AS is considered a rare disease, thin basement membrane is a frequent pattern, especially in families with a history of persistent hematuria. We report a patient with a diagnosis of AS who developed end-stage kidney disease (ESKD) and received a kidney transplant from a living unrelated donor. The graft biopsy specimen surprisingly showed a pattern of thin basement membranes.</AbstractText>
